================================================================================
  MYCORRHIZA  --  foray record
  joint scRNA-seq + spatial transcriptomics, one cross-modal embedding
================================================================================

collector .... ImmunoFounder working group
habitat ...... solid-tumour microenvironments, eleven cancer histologies
substrate .... dissociated single-cell RNA-seq coupled to tumour spatial spots
method ....... token-level cross-modal masked attention, one shared embedding
yield ........ thirty-two candidate immune-evasion programmes, nine spatially resolved


-- what is in this fruiting body ---------------------------------------------

The mycorrhiza package treats a dissociated cell and a spatial spot as two token
streams of the same organism. A single twelve-layer encoder mixes them with a
learnable modality-bias mask and a spatial adjacency bias, rotates spot tokens by
their tissue coordinates, and is trained to rebuild gene values that have been
hidden in either stream from the context left in both. After pretraining, the
unsupervised forage walk clusters the embedding, scores each cluster for immune
exclusion, and reads out a differential signature per flagged zone.

  mycorrhiza/substrate   corpus assembly, affine + modality tokeniser, rotary
                         coordinate encoding, k-nearest spatial graph, mutual
                         nearest pairs
  mycorrhiza/network     cross-modal masked attention, sparse mixture-of-experts
                         feed-forward, the encoder, the reconstruction head
  mycorrhiza/tropism     masked reconstruction, cross-modal alignment, spatial
                         coherence, the composite objective
  mycorrhiza/keys        macro-F1, concordance, adjusted Rand index, normalised
                         mutual information, response AUC, co-localisation F1,
                         DeLong, bootstrap interval, Cohen d, Benjamini-Hochberg
  mycorrhiza/cultivation trainer, optimiser, warmup-then-cosine schedule, atomic
                         seeded checkpoints
  mycorrhiza/foray       Leiden clustering, immune-exclusion score, programme
                         extraction and jaccard merge, zero-shot transfer
  mycorrhiza/identification   mutual-information lower bound, admissibility and
                         within-modality equivariance checks
  mycorrhiza/forager.py  the cultivate / key / forage / export entry points


-- collecting kit (installation) ---------------------------------------------

The bench was pinned to an older toolchain on purpose; do not float the versions.

  python -m venv .venv && . .venv/bin/activate
  pip install -r requirements.txt        # torch 1.13.1+cu117 and friends
  pip install -e .

A conda equivalent: python 3.8, torch 1.13.1 built against CUDA 11.7, then the
remaining wheels from requirements.txt.


-- substrate sources (data) --------------------------------------------------

Every download link is collected in cultures.txt; each was resolved live before
being recorded, and anything unreachable was left out. The dissociated corpus is
drawn from TISCH, HLCA and Tabula Sapiens; the spatial corpus from SODB and
STOmicsDB; the spatial-domain benchmark from spatialLIBD DLPFC; and the
checkpoint-response cell states from GEO GSE176021. Harmonisation keeps cells and
spots on a shared highly-variable gene set of at least five hundred genes, applies
library-size normalisation and a log transform, and rotates a 70/10/20 split over
five seeds.


-- cultivation (training) ----------------------------------------------------

  forager cultivate -c conf/symbiont.gin -e 100 -o runs/symbiont.pt

conf/symbiont.gin carries the reported settings: embedding 256, twelve layers,
eight heads, eight experts with top-two routing, mask fraction 0.30, objective
weights 0.5 and 0.3, AdamW at 5e-5 with weight decay 0.01, two-thousand-step
warmup into a cosine decay, gradient accumulation of eight, across four GPUs for an
effective batch of two thousand and forty-eight, one hundred passes over the
corpus. Ablation recipes for every reported variant sit next to it as
conf/ablation_*.gin; conf/_assay.gin is a shrunken recipe used only by the test
foray and must not be used for any reported number.


-- keying out (evaluation) ---------------------------------------------------

  forager key    -c conf/symbiont.gin     # spatial-domain ARI and NMI
  forager forage -c conf/symbiont.gin     # exclusion zones and merged programmes
  forager export -c conf/symbiont.gin -o runs/reconstructor.onnx

Reported reference points to aim for, mean over five seeds: pan-cancer cell-type
concordance 93.1 percent with macro-F1 0.871 and cancer AUROC 0.851; DLPFC spatial
domains ARI 0.641 and NMI 0.718; immune-evasion response AUC 0.78 with
co-localisation F1 0.709. The headline spatial niche couples TIGIT, CXCL13 and CCL5
at the tumour boundary and replicates across four external platforms with
co-localisation r between 0.82 and 0.93.


-- bench notes (compute budget) ----------------------------------------------

  parameters ....... about 120 million, 28.4 GB resident
  pretraining ...... 48 GPU-hours on four A100 80GB cards
  precision ........ fp32 by default; mixed precision is opt-in per recipe
  fine-tuning ...... about 35 minutes per downstream dataset
  inference ........ about 1,850 cells per second

These are not soft targets. The low learning rate and the hundred-pass schedule
make a full run long; the pinned CUDA 11.7 / torch 1.13.1 pairing is part of the
record and is what the numbers above were produced against.


-- vouchers (checkpoints) ----------------------------------------------------

Checkpoints are written atomically and carry their seed, step and epoch so a run
resumes exactly where it stopped. Trained weights are available on reasonable
request to the working group.


-- test foray ----------------------------------------------------------------

  pytest assays -q

The assays cover token shapes, within-modality equivariance, rotary relative-
coordinate invariance, an analytic-versus-finite-difference gradient, the loss
gates, the exclusion-score limits, the statistics against a reference, the
mutual-information bound, a single-batch overfit, and a two-step end-to-end run on
the assay recipe.
